Considering this: a binary background which contains small size contours already. Maybe we can do downsampling/blur operations in fast check, which can eliminate small contours in image? @AleksandrPanov 
The below code I used run more faster, like 1-2 seconds,  for test image of this issue. 
```
if (flags & CALIB_CB_FAST_CHECK)
{
    //perform new method for checking chessboard using a binary image.
    //image is binarised using a threshold dependent on the image histogram
    int max_cols = 1000;
    cv::Mat small_img;
    if (thresh_img_new.cols > max_cols)
    {
        small_img = thresh_img_new.clone();
        cv::resize(small_img, small_img, cv::Size(max_cols, img.rows * max_cols / (float)img.cols));
    }
    if (checkChessboardBinary(small_img, pattern_size) <= 0) //fall back to the old method
    {
        if (!checkChessboard(small_img, pattern_size))
        {
            corners_.release();
            return false;
        }
    }
}
```

Largely. For a background image containg textures like this:
```
uint8_t data[4][4] = {
	0,0,0,255,
	0,255,0,255,
	0,0,0,255,
	255,255,255,255
};
cv::Mat img(4, 4, 0, data);
cv::Mat small_img;
cv::resize(img, small_img, cv::Size(2, 2));
std::cout << small_img;
```
Any binarization algorithm will not change image and contour will exist. But a resize opteration will make these pixel values close to each other because of the interpolation part. The above code gives result
 [ 64, 128;
 128, 191].
For chessboard, since interpolation mainly influence the edge(values between white and black), which is negligible comparing with the grid area. @AleksandrPanov
